00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
larundan geraldine is still trying to  pick up belongings from the rubble of  her damaged home  a mother of five she says heavy rains in  chad's capital in germany forced her and  her family to seek shelter when their  roof and walls collapsed  i was in the market and it started  raining heavily then i got a call from  my neighbors that my house was destroyed  luckily my children were not home i came  back to find the situation like this  at least 22 people have been killed by  rains and floods and dozens of others  injured since the start of the season  according to the united nations  across the country over a thousand homes  have been destroyed  many people here say they've not seen  this much rain and destruction before  according to the u.n environmental  agency chad is one of the countries most  affected by climate change and those  likely to suffer as a result are the  vulnerable communities  the floods have left many with no access  to clean water this is one of the main  hospitals in germany now it's surrounded  by flood water  almer  because of the floods patients can't  come to the hospital there is no  transport we only receive severe cases  that are brought here by ambulances  but many of those who are sick can't  come  medical services have also been  disrupted amid concerns of waterborne  diseases  we only have two departments working  the children's department and the  surgery department all the other  departments have stopped working because  of the water in the hospital  the rains are expected to continue until  october and the fear is that until then  more homes will be destroyed more lives  lost heber morgan al jazeera in germany
 

